patch.body:
The tower is the western full stop of the Sloterhof, Jo Berghoef's ensemble of prefab flats for the Nemavo company, finished in 1958–1960. The rest of the complex is mostly long slabs set at right angles to the Comeniusstraat; this one stands alone on a near-square plan, with storage rooms on the ground floor and twelve floors of flats above. It was by far the tallest building in the area, and the view was used to advertise the flats. It even got its own address, on the Hemsterhuisstraat. Tenants on the north-west side look out over the Sloterplas.

The tower is clad in a darker stone than the walk-up blocks, with white vertical strips that pick out the balconies and white bands at each floor. Problems came soon: by 1966 the facades let in rain in strong winds. Tenants complained to Nemavo first, but it got so bad that the city's building inspectors were called in, and they ordered the cladding to be replaced so that people no longer had to live with their carpets rolled up.

Look for:
- the sgraffito by Jan Goeting at the entrance under the canopy (1960);
- the canopy itself, a striking detail;
- the white grid of balcony strips and floor bands against the darker panels.

The square honours Abraham Staalman (1871–1935), a pioneer of social housing and founder of the housing association Het Westen. Arthur Staal and Cornelis Keesman designed the complex around it in 1956–1958: at completion it counted 280 flats, 20 shops, 3 kiosks, 9 garages, two substations and a boiler house. The register guesses that the strict layout and facades are Keesman's, and that Staal, son of the architect J.F. Staal and later designer of the Shell tower on the IJ, added the playful parts: the staggered balconies and the three kiosks set at an angle between the Huizingalaan and the square. Some flats were built for families of thirteen, with three extra bedrooms in the semi-basement below.

Urban renewal since 2010 replaced much of the surrounding neighbourhood, and the low strip of shops that closed the south side is gone, so the square now opens onto the court behind it. The city listed the remaining hook of flats, the square and the kiosks in 2019 after a request from the heritage society Heemschut. It counts it, with the square at the Pieter Calandlaan and Meervaart, as the only largely intact square of the 1950s and 1960s in Nieuw-West.

The renewal also produced the neighbourhood's mascot. In 2011 Florentijn Hofman (of the giant Rubber Duck) built *Staalman* in the Staalmanpark, after working out the design with local young people: a bear of sprayed concrete over a steel frame, about ten metres tall. He looks tough, but carries a pillow. Hofman linked it to the advice that teenagers should go to bed on time instead of hanging around outside, and to the residents who had to pack up their belongings and move, sometimes twice, while their homes were rebuilt.

Look for:
- the pergola that ties the three kiosks together, resting on slender columns;
- slanting, staggered balconies on the square side, with the concrete floor bands running through them;
- the ground-floor brickwork: two courses of ordinary bricks alternating with one course of taller ones, and small square windows at different heights;
- Leo de Vries's sculpture of three stone blocks on the square, which with a little imagination becomes a reclining figure;
- the bear in the park, looking out over the neighbourhood like a gatekeeper.

After the war tuberculosis was claiming lives again, and Amsterdam set out to build "healthy" schools. Public Works architect Jan Leupen, with C. van der Wilk, developed a standard type that followed Jan Duiker's famous Open-Air School of 1930: two parallel blocks joined by two glazed passages, so the plan reads as an H. The classroom block has no corridors at all, so every classroom gets light and air from two sides; you walk through one classroom to reach the next. The low block holds the gym, a hall with a stage meant to double as a neighbourhood centre, and craft rooms. Ten were built up to 1960; only four survive, and three of them are here.

The Slotermeerschool (1953, originally the Burgemeester Fockschool) is the most intact, set back in a broad green strip. On the gym's end wall Frits Sieger's relief shows a boy and girl dancing among doves, fruit, wheat and sunflowers. The Burgemeester Röellschool on the Thomas van Aquinostraat, now De Kans, gained a new wing in 2011. The Burgemeester Rendorpschool on the Herman de Manstraat is now a school for deaf and hard-of-hearing children.

Look for:
- the tall ladder windows of the stairwells, running from ground to roof ridge;
- at the Slotermeerschool, three of Sieger's doves that have "flown out" of the relief and sit loose on the wall;
- at De Kans, *Jongen met haan* (1955), a bronze boy with a rooster on his head by Jan Wolkers, long before he became a famous novelist; it won him the Sint-Lucas medal.
